 { Black controls the only open file and is now ready to use it for an invasion. } 1... { -KEY- } Rd2 $1 2. Rbb2 { Logically enough, White keeps the crucial second rank under control. } Rxc2 3. Rxc2 Rd3 $1 { In the endgame, the reduced number of pieces often makes the third rank a possible working area for the rook. Note that this move becomes more efficient once one pair of rooks have left the board, since White will have more difficulties in protecting his pawns. } 4. Rb2 { Defending against the threat of 4...Rb3. } ( { a) Instead, } 4. Rc3 $2 { permitted } Rd2 $1 5. Ke3 Rb2 { with the double threat of 6...Rxb4 and 6...Nf4; } ) 
 ( { b) while the active } 4. b5 $5 { is well met by } Ra3 $1 5. b6 c5 6. Rd2 Nd4 ) 
Kc7 { A useful move, whereas White can hardly undertake anything. } 5. c5 Kd7 6. g3 $2 ( 6. Nc1 Rd1 7. Ne2 { was more tenacious, when Black could keep the pressure by } Nd4 { , intending } 8. Nxd4 exd4 { and ...Ke6-e5. } ) 
hxg3+ 7. hxg3 g4 $1 { A strong pawn break that soon causes a collapse of White's position. } 8. Ng1 ( { Or } 8. fxg4 Ng5 { , recovering the pawn at once. } ) 
Ng5 9. Ke2 Ra3 10. f4 { Paramount to suicide, but White was short of good options anyway. } Nxe4 11. fxe5 fxe5 12. Kf1 Nxg3+ 13. Kg2 Nh5 14. Rf2 Nf4+ 15. Kh1 Ra1 16. Rd2+ Ke7 
 0-1

