 { Black is about to seize the second rank by ...Rc2, but it is White to move, and he now exploits the e-file in order to seize the seventh rank. } 1. { -KEY- } f3 $1 Bg6 2. Re7 $1 b6 $6 ( { Probably } 2... Rc2 { was better, although after } 3. Rae1 h6 4. R1e2 $5 { (another promising option is } 4. Rxb7 Rxb2 5. Ree7 { - Kasparov) } Rxe2 5. Bxe2 b6 6. Rc7 { , followed by Kf2, White keeps some pressure. } ) 
3. Rae1 h6 ( { With } 3... Rc2 $4 { obviously failing to } 4. Re8 { , Black has to spend a tempo on yet another pawn move. } ) 
4. Rb7 $1 { The rook vacates e7 to its colleague with tempo. } Rd6 ( { Or } 4... Rc2 5. Ree7 { , when both } Rxb2 ( { and } 5... Kf8 6. f4 $1 ) 
6. Be8 $1 { let White keep a strong initiative. } ) 
5. Ree7 h5 ( { This time } 5... Rc2 { permits } 6. Re8 Kh7 7. Rbb8 $1 { , increasing the pressure - Karpov. } ) 
6. gxh5 Bxh5 7. g4 $1 { The next step in White's plan is to advance his kingside pawns to deflect the enemy bishop from the defence of the f7-pawn. } ( 7. Be8 $6 Rc1 8. Kf2 Rc2 { was less convincing. } ) 
Bg6 8. f4 $1 Rc1+ 9. Kf2 Rc2+ 10. Ke3 Be4 ( { Or } 10... Re6 11. Rxe6 fxe6 12. Rxb6 { and wins - Karpov. } ) 
11. Rxf7 Rg6 12. g5 Kh7 13. Rfe7 $1 { Preparing Be8. } ( { a) } 13. Be8 $1 { at once was also strong; } ) 
 ( { b) but not } 13. f5 $6 Rxg5 14. f6 Rf5 ) 
Rxb2 14. Be8 Rb3+ 15. Ke2 Rb2+ 16. Ke1 Rd6 ( { Or } 16... Rb1 17. Kd2 Rb2 18. Kc3 Rc2 19. Kb3 { etc. - Karpov. } ) 
17. Rxg7+ Kh8 18. Rge7 { Black resigned - seeing that, when his checks are over, the retreat of White's bishop will produce decisive mating threats. } 
 1-0

