 { In this double rook ending Black had pinned his hopes on the trade of the queenside pawns, thus reaching a structure with four pawns vs. three on the kingside. However, Petrosian had other plans. } 1. { -KEY- } Rd1 $1 { Heading for d7. } Rxa3 2. g3 Ra8 $2 { Underestimating the danger on the seventh rank. } ( { By } 2... Ra6 $1 3. Rdd7 $6 ( { instead, White should prefer } 2... Ra8 2. Rc1 Ra4 4. Rc7 Rexc4 5. Rxc5 { , transposing to the above-mentioned ending. } ) 
Rf6 { Black could have defended the f7-pawn in a more active way - Tal. } ) 
3. Rdd7 $1 Rf8 4. Rbc7 Rxc4 5. Kg2 { Material is even, but White is much better thanks to his pressure along the seventh rank and the passive enemy rook on f8. Next, his plan is to advance e3-e4-e5-e6, creating a mating motif if the black king remains on the back rank. } h5 6. h3 Kg7 7. Kf3 Kf6 8. h4 Kg7 9. Re7 $1 { Facilitating the advance of the e-pawn. } Kf6 10. e4 Rd4 11. e5+ Kf5 ( 11... Kg7 $2 { would run into } 12. e6 ) 
12. Rxc5 { The endgame with four vs. three on the kingside is finally reached, but in quite an unusual version where Black is still suffering from the pressure at f7. Besides, the double pair of rooks means that his king is exposed to mating motifs. } Rd3+ 13. Kg2 Ra3 14. Rc6 { Heading for f6. } ( { Equally interesting was } 14. Rc4 $5 g5 { (mate was threatened) } 15. hxg5 Kxg5 16. Rf4 Kg6 17. Rf6 Kg7 18. Kh3 { , followed by f2-f4 and Kh4. } ) 
Ke4 15. Rf6 Raa8 16. e6 $1 ( { Not } 16. Rfxf7 $2 Rxf7 17. Rxf7 Kxe5 ) 
Ke5 ( { Obviously, } 16... fxe6 $2 17. Rexe6 { would lead to the loss of the g6-pawn. } ) 
17. Rfxf7 Rg8 18. Rb7 $5 { An interesting choice - Petrosian abandons the e-pawn in exchange for a positional advantage: a cut-off enemy king. } ( 18. Rg7 $2 Rxg7 19. Rxg7 Kf6 { was less to the point } ) 
 ( { though by } 18. f4 $5 Kd6 19. Rg7 { , White could improve that variation, with excellent winning chances; e.g. } Rab8 20. Rb7 $1 Ra8 ( { or } 20. Rb7 Kxe6 21. Rxg6 ) 
21. Rxg8 Rxg8 22. e7 { , followed by Kf3-e4. } ) 
Kxe6 19. Rbe7+ Kd6 20. Re2 Raf8 21. Ra7 Ra8 22. Rae7 Rac8 23. R2e6+ { Preparing a curious, but strong plan: Kh3, f2-f4, Ra6 and Ree6 with a decisive attack at g6. } Kd5 24. Kh3 Rc7 $2 { A grave error in a difficult position. } 25. Rxg6 $1 { Seeing that he will end up two pawns down, Black resigned. } 
 1-0

